      "program_name": "Welfare Exemption (property tax), Yolo County Assessor",
      "administering_agency": "Yolo County Assessor/Clerk-Recorder/Elections (ACE), Jesse Salinas, 625 Court Street, Woodland, CA 95695; (530) 666-8135; ace.yolocounty.gov; with the State Board of Equalization (Organizational Clearance Certificate)",
      "funding_type": "tax abatement",
      "typical_award_size": "Exemption of property taxes on units restricted to lower income households under Revenue and Taxation Code 214(g): full exemption for qualifying units in LIHTC or publicly financed projects and partial exemption for the qualifying share in mixed projects. In Yolo County the 1% base rate plus voter-approved debt applies to the exempt value.",
      "per_unit_cap": "Statewide $20 million assessed-value cap on the 214(g) exemption for non-LIHTC projects relying only on a regulatory agreement; no cap for LIHTC projects. AB 84 (2024) allows a share of units occupied by households up to 100% AMI to remain exempt, reported on BOE-267-L4.",
      "affordability_requirement": "Nonprofit owner or limited partnership with a nonprofit managing general partner holding a BOE Organizational Clearance Certificate; recorded regulatory agreement (tax credits, bonds, HOME, PLHA, housing authority or County agreement) restricting rents to lower income households; BOE-267-L2 tenant data where there is no government financing or tax credits.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "new construction",
        "acquisition-rehab",
        "preservation",
        "supportive housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "annual NOFA",
      "next_deadline": "File BOE-267 (first year) or BOE-267-A (annual) with the Assessor by February 15 each year with BOE-267-L, L1, L2 and L-A supplements as applicable; late filings through December 31 receive a partial exemption. The Yolo ACE website does not post exemption instructions; call the Assessor's office.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "false",
      "layering_notes": "Standard underwriting item. Yolo County Housing is actively using the exemption as a financing tool: its FY 2026 plan commits to working with private investors to build affordable workforce housing 'utilizing the benefits of the welfare exemption,' and the December 2025 KIND West Sacramento deal deed-restricted 30 units for moderate income households with a YCH operating loan structured to meet exemption requirements. Check carefully that moderate income (above 80% AMI) units qualify only within the AB 84 over-income allowance; the core exemption is for lower income units.",
